WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.340
Smart robots are no longer the stuff of science fiction.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.160
But intelligence is more

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.060
than besting humans at board games.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:11.360
It's basic physical skills, too —

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.960
like turning door knob or opening a bag.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.080
Babies take  sensory information

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.900
from the world around them to figure out

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.020
how to use their bodies and interact.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.560
Robots aren't quite as sophisticated.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.780
In the past, they've failed pretty epically

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.900
at controlling their bodies.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.020
But new improvements could go a long way

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.260
to help machines sense the physical world and

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.660
learn new skills based on what they see, feel or hear.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.120
Some senses are more easily mastered than others.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.200
If you have an iPhone you might interact with Siri,

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.320
an acoustically astute artificial intelligence

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.160
that picks up on your speech patterns

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.660
to better respond to questions.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.680
"Hi, Siri, are you alive?"

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.280
"In the cloud, no one questions your existential status."

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.620
Other senses are trickier.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:57.590
Take touch.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.079
Humans are bombarded with tactile information

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.540
through skin —

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.340
temperature, pressure, texture...

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.280
Researchers at the University of Colorado

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.680
took a similar approach with a robot named Baxter.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.100
They gave it smart skin

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.620
that translates a flood of vibration data

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.380
into simple messages.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.140
Baxter's sensors can differentiate between

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.820
15 different textures.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.580
Touch sensors also need to be tough.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.040
SAIL-R, a bot developed by a team at Stanford

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.900
wears durable sensors on twirling legs.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.720
Soon it may be able to tell hard ground

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.180
from grass or sand —

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.320
and adjust the way it scrambles forward.

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:37.250
But there's a difference between sensing

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:38.499
the world around you

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.780
and actually doing something with that information.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.450
Researchers at UCLA gave their robot

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.770
squishy sensitive finger pads

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:48.910
and a plastic bag full of cheerios.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.220
The robot actually figured out how to close the bag —

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:53.000
on its own.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.000
Touch is a handy skill,

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.900
but staying upright is a pretty important one.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.799
Your inner ear, eyes and feet

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.460
all  give you a sense of balance.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.320
Some robots, like Atlas, are equipped with

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.340
body and leg sensors that do the same thing.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.620
Atlas might take a tumble now and then,

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.380
but a new upgrade enables the bot to get right back up.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.020
So that's hearing, touch, balance...

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.590
What about sight?

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.120
Lasers can tell a robot how far it is

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.660
from an object or obstacle

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.920
Google's driverless car also uses

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.200
lasers, radar, cameras and software

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.800
to recognize the shape of a pedestrian

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.560
or the outstretched arm of a cyclist.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.400
Other robots tackle even more complex visual challenges.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.680
Star is a surgical robot with senses

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.220
that go beyond human abilities

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.540
It has 3-D and near infrared cameras —

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.460
like wearing night-vision goggles.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.700
Star can see glowing dots marked by humans on tissue,

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.740
calculate a stitch plan and sew up a live animal —

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.140
and maybe one day a human patient.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.220
Star's makers hope to add more superhuman senses,

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.800
like measuring blood.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.040
Things that seem like baby steps for humans

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.280
are giant leaps for robotkind,

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:13.580
especially robots designed to work in the real world.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.840
Stickybot, for example, is a gecko-inspired robot

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.640
that uses special toe pads to scale walls.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.019
MicroTugs are teeny tiny robots that can haul

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.040
materials hundreds of times their body weight.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.300
Smart physical sensors can make these bots

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.520
more aware of their environment

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.420
and allow them to focus on more complex tasks.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.080
One day, such sensitive robots might not work for us

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.740
or against us...

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.860
but alongside us.

